In regions where bands for different particles merge together, the \(dE/dx\) fitting is less reliable. A notable case in point is electron contamination. To solve this problem, for each particle type, amplitudes at the band center (heights of the Gaussians from the multi-Gaussian fitting) were re-fitted as a function of momentum. Fig. 7.4 shows such a fitting. In the plot, the amplitude of the electron band center in the clean region (\(0.2<p<0.4\) GeV\(/c\)) is fitted by an exponential function, then extrapolated to the merged region (\(0.1<p<0.2\) and \(0.4<p<0.6\) GeV\(/c\)). For the region where \(p<0.1\) GeV\(/c\), the electron band and pion band are well separated, so their PID can be performed by simply cutting on \(dE/dx\).

